"Big Ben is a giant bell inside a tall clock tower in London. It goes BONG!": "L0153.mp3",
"'Big Ben' is really the name of the huge bell inside the clock tower in London. The tower was finished in 1859, and the bell weighs about as much as two elephants!": "L0154.mp3",
"Word of the place: tower, which means a tall, narrow building.": "L0155.mp3",
"This big building by the river is where leaders meet to make rules.": "L0156.mp3",
"The Houses of Parliament sit beside the River Thames. People chosen by voters meet here to talk about and make the country's laws.": "L0157.mp3",
"Word of the place: government, which means the group of people who run a country.": "L0158.mp3",
"Tower Bridge can open in the middle to let tall ships go by!": "L0159.mp3",
"Tower Bridge opened in 1894. Its road splits and lifts up in the middle so tall ships can sail through on the Thames.": "L0160.mp3",
"Word of the place: bascule, which means a bridge part that swings up like a seesaw.": "L0161.mp3",
"The River Thames flows right through London.": "L0162.mp3",
"The River Thames is England's most famous river. It flows through London and out to the North Sea.": "L0163.mp3",
"Word of the place: river, which means a large stream of water that flows to the sea.": "L0164.mp3",
"A palace is a giant home for a king or queen.": "L0165.mp3",
"London's royal palace has hundreds of rooms. Guards in tall fuzzy hats stand outside, and a flag shows when the King is home.": "L0166.mp3",
"Word of the place: palace, which means a huge, grand home for a royal family.": "L0167.mp3",
"The Big Wheel goes round slowly so you can see far away!": "L0168.mp3",
"London's giant observation wheel turns so slowly that people step on while it moves. From the top you can see across the whole city.": "L0169.mp3",
"Word of the place: observation, which means watching carefully.": "L0170.mp3",
"A double-decker bus has two floors! Sit upstairs to see more.": "L0171.mp3",
"London's red double-decker buses have two levels. They have carried passengers around the city for about 100 years.": "L0172.mp3",
"Word of the place: passenger, which means a person riding in a vehicle.": "L0173.mp3",
"Castles have thick stone walls to keep people safe.": "L0174.mp3",
"England has many castles. Windsor Castle is the oldest castle in the world that people still live in, and it's over 900 years old!": "L0175.mp3",
"Word of the place: fortress, which means a strong, protected building.": "L0176.mp3",
"A village green is a grassy park where everyone can play.": "L0177.mp3",
"Many English villages have a 'green', a shared grassy space for fairs, games of cricket, and celebrations.": "L0178.mp3",
"Word of the place: community, which means a group of people who live in the same place.": "L0179.mp3",
"These cliffs are white because they are made of chalk!": "L0180.mp3",
"The White Cliffs of Dover are made of chalk, which formed from tiny sea creatures' shells over millions of years.": "L0181.mp3",
"Word of the place: chalk, which means a soft white rock.": "L0182.mp3",
"A lighthouse shines a bright light so boats stay safe at night.": "L0183.mp3",
"Lighthouses flash light out to sea so sailors can find their way and stay away from rocks.": "L0184.mp3",
"Word of the place: beacon, which means a light or signal that guides people.": "L0185.mp3",
"Hawaii's islands were made by volcanoes!": "L0186.mp3",
"Every Hawaiian island was built by volcanoes pushing up lava from the ocean floor. Mauna Kea is taller than Mount Everest if you measure from the sea floor!": "L0187.mp3",
"Word of the place: volcano, which means a mountain where hot melted rock comes out.": "L0188.mp3",
"Diamond Head is an old volcano crater near the beach.": "L0189.mp3",
"Diamond Head is a crater from a volcano that erupted about 300,000 years ago. Sailors once thought its shiny rocks were diamonds!": "L0190.mp3",
"Word of the place: crater, which means a big bowl-shaped hole.": "L0191.mp3",
"Waikiki is a famous beach for swimming and surfing.": "L0192.mp3",
"Waikiki means 'spouting fresh water' in Hawaiian. Hawaiian royalty surfed here long ago, and now visitors come from all over the world.": "L0193.mp3",
"Word of the place: tradition, which means something people have done for a long time.": "L0194.mp3",
"Hotels are where visitors sleep on vacation.": "L0195.mp3",
"Tall hotels line Waikiki Beach so visitors can wake up and see the ocean from their balconies.": "L0196.mp3",
"Word of the place: visitor, which means a person who comes to see a place.": "L0197.mp3",
"This beach restaurant is named after Duke, a famous surfer and swimmer.": "L0198.mp3",
"Duke Kahanamoku was a Hawaiian swimmer who won Olympic gold medals and shared surfing with the world. He's called the father of modern surfing.": "L0199.mp3",
"Word of the place: champion, which means the winner of a contest.": "L0200.mp3",
"This street is full of fun shops.": "L0201.mp3",
"Kalakaua Avenue is Waikiki's main street, named after King Kalakaua, Hawaii's 'Merrie Monarch' who loved music and hula.": "L0202.mp3",
"Word of the place: avenue, which means a wide street.": "L0203.mp3",
"A surf break is where waves are good for surfing.": "L0204.mp3",
"A surf break is a spot where waves curl and break in a way that's good for surfing, often over a reef or sandbar.": "L0205.mp3",
"Word of the place: reef, which means a ridge of rock or coral near the surface of the sea.": "L0206.mp3",
"In winter the North Shore has giant waves!": "L0207.mp3",
"Oahu's North Shore gets huge winter waves, sometimes taller than a house, and the world's best surfers come to ride them.": "L0208.mp3",
"Word of the place: swell, which means big, rolling ocean waves that come from far away.": "L0209.mp3",
"Food trucks sell yummy food right by the beach.": "L0210.mp3",
"North Shore shrimp trucks became famous for garlic shrimp served with rice. Food trucks are like tiny kitchens on wheels.": "L0211.mp3",
"Word of the place: menu, which means a list of food you can order.": "L0212.mp3",
"Snorkelers float and look at fish under the water.": "L0213.mp3",
"Hanauma Bay is a curved bay made from an old volcano crater. Its coral reef is protected, so fish and turtles are safe there.": "L0214.mp3",
"Word of the place: marine, which means having to do with the sea.": "L0215.mp3",
"These green mountains have pointy ridges like folded paper.": "L0216.mp3",
"The Koolau Range is what's left of an ancient volcano. Rain and wind carved its steep, pleated green cliffs.": "L0217.mp3",
"Word of the place: erosion, which means wearing away of rock by water and wind.": "L0218.mp3",
"A lagoon is calm water protected by a reef.": "L0219.mp3",
"A reef can block big waves and make a calm lagoon behind it, a safe home for baby fish.": "L0220.mp3",
"Word of the place: lagoon, which means calm water separated from the sea.": "L0221.mp3",
"Water falls down the cliff into a pool.": "L0222.mp3",
"Hawaii's mountains catch lots of rain, so waterfalls tumble down the green cliffs into pools below.": "L0223.mp3",
"Word of the place: cascade, which means a small waterfall.": "L0224.mp3",
"A rainforest gets lots of rain, so plants grow big!": "L0225.mp3",
"Rainforests get so much rain that plants grow in layers, from the forest floor all the way up to the treetops.": "L0226.mp3",
"Word of the place: canopy, which means the top layer of leaves in a forest.": "L0227.mp3",
"A lei is a necklace of flowers that says 'welcome' and 'I love you'.": "L0228.mp3",
"In Hawaii, a lei is given to show love, welcome, or congratulations. May 1st is Lei Day!": "L0229.mp3",
"Word of the place: aloha, which means a Hawaiian word for hello, goodbye, and love.": "L0230.mp3",
"A tiki hut has a grass roof to make shade.": "L0231.mp3",
"Thatched huts use dried leaves and grasses for roofs, which keep out sun and rain.": "L0232.mp3",
"Word of the place: thatch, which means a roof made of dried plants.": "L0233.mp3",
